# Postgres connection (Supabase pooler URL or local). Required by the dashboard
# and by the scripts in scripts/.
TCA_DATABASE_URL=postgresql://user:pass@host:6543/postgres

# QuickNode archive endpoint for Base mainnet. debug_traceTransaction + eth_call
# at historical block heights require an archive plan, AND the trace methods
# require QuickNode's paid Trace Mode add-on — it is off by default, and without
# it the decoder produces no receipts at all rather than degrading. Required.
#
# QuickNode also caps eth_getLogs at a 10,000-block RANGE (HTTP 413 past it, no
# matter how few logs match); refreshReactors.ts CHUNK_BLOCKS is sized to that.
#
# Note: the RPC e2e tests skip silently unless this is EXPORTED, and `source .env`
# does not export. Use: set -a && source .env && set +a
TCA_RPC_URL=https://YOUR-ENDPOINT.base-mainnet.quiknode.pro/YOUR_TOKEN/

# ── Access gate (protects /trades and DELETE only) ───────────────────────────
# The receipt tool is PUBLIC by design: anyone can paste a hash on the index and
# generate a receipt, no password. The gate protects /trades — the shared
# history of everything everyone has analyzed — and DELETE, the one destructive
# endpoint. Those fail CLOSED: with either value unset they return 503 rather
# than opening. The public tool keeps working either way, so a missing env var
# never takes the product down, only closes what the password was guarding.
#
# APP_ACCESS_PASSWORD is what you type on /login.
# APP_SESSION_SECRET signs the session cookie — rotate it to log everyone out.
# Generate one with: openssl rand -base64 32
APP_ACCESS_PASSWORD=
APP_SESSION_SECRET=

# ── Rate limits ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# ⚠️ Because POST /api/receipts is public, these are the ONLY thing between an
# anonymous visitor and the RPC bill. A fresh analysis is ~40 RPC calls.
#
# Per client IP, per minute:
#   ANALYSES  — fresh analyses (the expensive path; cache hits do not count)
#   REQUESTS  — cheaper overall ceiling that also covers cache hits
#   DIAGNOSIS — the GET /?tx= path, which spends RPC on a miss and needs no JS
# RATE_LIMIT_ANALYSES_PER_MIN=20
# RATE_LIMIT_REQUESTS_PER_MIN=120
# RATE_LIMIT_DIAGNOSIS_PER_MIN=30
#
# Across ALL clients, per hour. This is the circuit breaker: per-IP limits are
# walkable by simply using more IPs, so this is the only ceiling a distributed
# flood cannot get around. Blunt on purpose — when it trips, new analyses pause
# for everyone rather than quietly running up an invoice. At ~40 calls each, 500
# caps a worst-case hour near 20k RPC calls.
# RATE_LIMIT_ANALYSES_GLOBAL_PER_HOUR=500
#
# NOTE: these counters live in process memory, which is correct for a single
# container (Railway) but NOT for a multi-instance/serverless deploy — there,
# each instance keeps its own counters and every limit above (including the
# global one) multiplies by the instance count. Swap the store in
# lib/rateLimit.ts for Redis before scaling out.

# Optional. Free Etherscan V2 key, used to name verified fee-sink contracts on
# the receipt (getsourcecode). Without it those lines fall back to a generic
# "[Aggregator] Fee" label — nothing else degrades.
ETHERSCAN_API_KEY=

# Optional, currently inert. The Dune ETH/USD oracle is wired up but disabled:
# DUNE_ETH_USD_QUERY_ID in packages/core/src/duneOracle.ts is still 0. Setting
# this key alone does nothing.
# DUNE_API_KEY=

# ── Outbound webhooks (Slack/Discord incoming-webhook URL) ──────────────────
# ⚠️ Local and production share ONE database, so a receipt generated while
# testing locally is a real receipt. Setting these locally posts it to the
# same Slack channel as production traffic. Set them on the deployment, not
# in your local .env.
#
# Two independent streams — an unset URL degrades to a log line and NEVER
# falls back to the other stream's URL:
#   ALERT_WEBHOOK_URL    — incidents only (the global spend ceiling), debounced
#                           to one message per hour so a flood can't turn the
#                           alert itself into an outage.
#   ACTIVITY_WEBHOOK_URL — one message per newly generated receipt, not
#                           debounced. Separate destination on purpose: during
#                           a flood, activity volume would bury the ceiling
#                           warning exactly when it matters most.
# ALERT_WEBHOOK_URL=
# ACTIVITY_WEBHOOK_URL=

# Optional. The base URL used to build the receipt link in the
# ACTIVITY_WEBHOOK_URL message (e.g. https://your-app.up.railway.app). Without
# it the link is derived from the request's Host header, which is
# caller-controlled on this public endpoint — a forged Host could otherwise
# land a spoofed link in the team's own Slack. Set once a domain is settled.
# APP_BASE_URL=
